course: everyday
lesson: 412
title: In-game purchases and skins: the dancing banana problem
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kai got forty pounds for his birthday and spent all of it, that same evening, on a skin that turned his Fortnite player into a dancing banana. For one glorious day Kai was the funniest thing in the squad: the banana danced at the start of every match, the banana danced when it won, the banana danced when it lost. A week later the banana was just a banana. Kai looked at the Lego set he had wanted, at forty pounds, in the shop window, and said a word his mum pretended not to hear.
explain: Priya did something cleverer before she bought her dream skin. Her mum paid her for hoovering, so she worked out what the skin really cost: not «just 1,500 V-bucks», but twelve hours of hoovering the stairs. Priya looked at the stairs. The stairs looked at Priya. She saved for a bike instead. Ollie, asked by his mum how much he had spent on games that year, guessed «about five pounds». They checked the purchase history together: forty-two pounds, in little bits of 79p and £1.99 that he had never added up.
explain: Leo swore a new skin made him play better, and lost in the new skin exactly the way he had lost in the old one. Theo was teased for playing in the free default skin — «no-skin», the others called him — until Mia announced that default skins were «stealth mode», and the whole squad wore them for a week. Jayden bought a skin because his favourite YouTuber wore it, then found out the YouTuber had been paid to show it off: it was an advert dressed up as a friend's tip.
explain: Games use quiet tricks to make spending feel easy. Prices are in coins, gems or V-bucks instead of pounds, so they feel like play money. Ruby's battle pass gave rewards only if she played every day (when she missed a few days she got fewer rewards, and found she did not really mind), and Noah's building game made him wait hours unless he spent gems — and when the gems ran out, it offered to sell him more for real money. Mia's answer was a simple rule of her own: only half of any money goes on games. By summer the other half had paid for a day at the water park, where nobody cared what skin you had.
fact: A skin changes how your character looks, not how well you play.
fact: Game coins, gems and V-bucks are bought with real money, even though they look like play money.
fact: Small purchases add up: ten spends of £1.99 cost almost £20.
fact: Turning a price into hours of chores shows what a thing really costs you.
fact: The excitement of a new skin usually fades within days.
fact: Some games make you wait on purpose, so you will pay to stop waiting.
fact: Rewards for playing every day are made to keep you coming back, even when you do not feel like it.
fact: YouTubers are sometimes paid to show off items; in the UK and USA such videos should say they are adverts.
fact: Most consoles and app stores show a purchase history that a parent can check.
fact: Waiting a few days before a big spend helps you see whether you really want it.
fact: Teasing someone for what they cannot or do not buy is unkind and says nothing about the person.
exercise: How did Kai feel the day he bought the banana skin? | Amazing, like the funniest in the squad | Guilty from the very first minute he wore it | Bored, he did not like bananas | Angry that it cost so much
exercise: What happened a week after Kai bought the banana? | It felt boring and he wished he had the Lego | It became even funnier every day | He sold it back for forty pounds | The game gave him a second banana for free
exercise: How did Priya find out what her dream skin really cost? | She turned the price into hours of hoovering | She asked a YouTuber what it was really worth | She waited until the skin was free for everyone | She counted up all her friends' skins at school
exercise: What did Priya decide after her sum? | To save for a bike instead | To hoover for twelve hours straight | To borrow the money from Kai | To buy two skins to save time
exercise: Ollie guessed he had spent five pounds. What was the real total? | Forty-two pounds in little bits | Exactly five pounds, just as he thought | Nothing, it was all free in the end | About four hundred pounds in all
exercise: Why are small spends easy to miss? | Each one feels tiny, but they add up | Small spends are not counted as real money | Games refund small spends later in the month | Parents never see small spends
exercise: Leo said a new skin made him play better. What really happened? | He lost the same way as in the old skin | He won every game that week | The skin gave him extra speed in every match | The skin made him play worse
exercise: Why did Theo feel embarrassed? | He was teased for using the free default skin | He lost a match to Mia | He had spent far too much of his own money on skins | His skin was a banana
exercise: What did Mia do when Theo was teased? | Called default skins «stealth mode» so the squad wore them | Bought Theo an expensive skin | Told the teasers to go away, then left the squad and played alone | Told a teacher the next day
exercise: What did Jayden find out about the YouTuber's skin? | The YouTuber was paid to show it | The YouTuber had designed the skin himself | The skin was free for everyone to use | The YouTuber had never used it in a game
exercise: Why do games use coins and gems instead of pounds? | So spending feels like play money, not real money | Because pounds and pence do not work inside games | Because coins are cheaper than pounds | So children can play without paying
exercise: What happened when Noah's gems ran out? | The game offered to sell him more for real money | The game gave him more gems for free, as a present | The building finished on its own straight away | The game closed and deleted his town
exercise: Ruby's battle pass rewards only came if she played every day. What did she find when she missed days? | She got fewer rewards and did not really mind | She lost her whole account and all her progress | Her friends stopped talking to her | The game sent her a fine
exercise: What was Mia's spending rule? | Only half of any money goes on games | All money goes on games at once, the day it arrives | Never spend anything on games, ever | Spend only on Fridays
exercise: What is wise before spending a lot on a skin? | Wait a few days and see if you still want it | Buy it fast before you change your mind | Buy two in case one breaks | Ask a stranger online whether it is worth it
exercise: Which is a kind of courage in this lesson? | Wearing the default skin when others tease | Spending all your money to fit in | Teasing others first so they don't tease you | Hiding what you spent from your parents
test: «nah mate this skin makes me cracked, I'm way better now». true? | No, a skin changes how you look, not how you play | Yes, rare skins are faster and jump a bit higher | Yes, if it cost a lot | Only in the first week
test: «only 79p, doesn't count». Ollie does it twenty times. What's true? | It adds up to almost sixteen pounds | It still costs only 79p altogether | Small spends are always free | Twenty spends cost the same as one
test: Theo's mate: «lol default skin, are u poor or what». Best reply in the squad? | What you spend says nothing about who you are | Default skins mean you are bad at games | He should buy a skin to stop the teasing | Laugh along and tease someone else instead
test: Jayden: «my fave YouTuber's got it, so it must be good». What should he wonder? | Was the YouTuber paid to show it? | Is the YouTuber's skin bigger than his? | Did the YouTuber pay double? | Is the skin heavier than others?
test: «just 1,500 V-bucks, that's nothing». What does Priya say? | V-bucks cost real money, so check the price in pounds | V-bucks are free, so spend them all whenever you like | V-bucks are worth nothing at all | V-bucks only count after midnight
test: the game: «wait 8 hours or finish NOW for 50 gems!» What's going on? | The game makes waiting annoying so you pay | The game is being kind and saving you time | Gems make the game run faster | Waiting breaks the game
test: Ruby: «gotta log in every day or I lose my rewards, ugh». Wise thought? | If a game makes you play out of worry, ask who's in charge | She must never miss a day, even when she's ill in bed | She should play all night to get ahead | She should buy two passes
test: Kai, birthday money in hand: «buying it NOW». Wisest advice? | Wait a few days and see if he still wants it | Buy it quick before the money disappears | Spend it all, money is for spending | Buy the banana and the Lego together
test: Ollie's mum: «how much this year on games?» What's the best way to answer truthfully? | Check the purchase history together | Guess a small number and hope she believes it | Say «nothing» and change the subject | Ask a friend to guess how much he spent
test: Mia's rule: half on games, half saved. She gets £20. How much for games? | Ten pounds | Twenty pounds | Five pounds | Fifteen pounds
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the denomination of in-game prices in coins, gems or similar units is considered to influence the expenditure of minors. | Such units obscure the fact that real money is being spent | Such units are cheaper than the national currency | Such units are refundable upon request in all cases | Such units are exempt from any purchase history
exam: Whereas a pupil incurs twenty separate charges of seventy-nine pence each, determine the aggregate expenditure. | Fifteen pounds and eighty pence | Seventy-nine pence | Seven pounds and ninety pence | Twenty pounds and seventy-nine pence
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a cosmetic item, hereinafter a skin, upon a player's performance? | It alters appearance and does not alter skill | It improves skill in direct proportion to its price | It impairs skill where the item is free | It alters skill solely during the first week
exam: Select the response most consistent with established guidance in respect of a large intended purchase of a cosmetic item. | The purchase ought to be deferred for several days and reconsidered | The purchase ought to be completed without delay, lest the price rise | The purchase ought to be doubled to obtain value | The purchase ought to be concealed from a parent
